<p class="wp-block-paragraph">One of many unexpected benefits of <a href="https://randsinrepose.com/archives/ask-rands/">Ask Rands</a> is that having a robot answer questions from the internet about all of your writing helps you discover contradictions. I&#8217;ve flip-flopped on whether a leader should code, and <a href="https://randsinrepose.com/archives/how-grumbles-helps/">Grumbles</a> knows this and <a href="https://randsinrepose.com/ask/?q=Has+Rands+flip+flopped+on+coding+as+a+manager%3F">explains it</a>:</p>



<blockquote class="wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow"><p>Yes, and he did it out loud. In &#8220;Technicality&#8221; (2007), Rands lays out his old new-manager rule, &#8220;Stop coding,&#8221; then says: &#8220;Too bad I&#8217;m wrong.&#8221; He adds, &#8220;Not totally,&#8221; and jokes he might need to call past co-workers to apologize. That&#8217;s not a flip-flop so much as a signed confession.</p></blockquote>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Another evolution. I stopped using the word managers and started using the word leaders. This is problematic, as the book most folks know is called &#8220;Managing Humans.&#8221; Gotta say it — love that punchy title, but how does that square with one of my favorite pithy sayings: &#8220;Managers tell you where you are. Leaders tell you where you are going&#8221;?</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">So, which is it, Rands? Leaders or managers?</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Wait for it.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">The End of the Trilogy</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I&#8217;m delighted (relieved) to report that I wrote another book. I&#8217;m calling it the end of the trilogy because the first O&#8217;Reilly book, <a href="https://link.amazon/B06VfuyKH">Software Developer&#8217;s Career Handbook</a>, focuses on getting into leadership; the second one, <a href="https://amzn.to/3eq8ACY">The Art of Leadership</a>, worked on second-level managers often known as Directors, and <a href="https://amzn.to/3SCULKE">this one</a> focuses on a day in the life of the senior leaders, the Executives.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">There was no grand plan to do a trilogy. The writing mostly comes from this blog, and over the years that I wrote, I was a front-line manager, a Director, and an Executive. The writing reflects the experience.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><a href="https://amzn.to/3SCULKE">A Day in the Life</a> contains the most original writing of any of the books, including original sections for your Morning, Afternoon, Evening, and Night. I had a large amount of help from Grumbles, who wrote none of the words, but was incredibly handy with technical and organizational issues. Thanks, Grumbles!</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I&#8217;d also like to thank <a href="https://charity.wtf/">Charity Majors</a>, who wrote the foreword for this book; my favorite line:</p>



<blockquote class="wp-block-quote is-layout-flow wp-block-quote-is-layout-flow"><p>Stories train the LLM between your ears.</p></blockquote>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">It&#8217;s Both</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you read my pithy saying as a ding on managers, you&#8217;re right. That was the original intent. At the time, I was frustrated with the managers who just managed. Just told me where we were. Assessed the current situation. And didn&#8217;t seem to care about the future. Leadership, the act of imagining a better future and figuring out how to get there, seemed much more relevant and helpful.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">It&#8217;s both. There are times when you need to manage and an equal number of times when you need to lead. A great manager is who I need to be when the sky is starting to fall. This is a human who knows how to handle the disaster. They aren&#8217;t going to prevent it, but that&#8217;s work for later when everyone has calmed down.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">And then there are times we require a great leader. Close your eyes and imagine a leader you admire. Tell me a story about them. I bet you have a story about an awe-inspiring accomplishment or perhaps a product they built. Or advice they gave you at just the right time.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">These impressive leaders — they are just humans&#8230; like you. What they do differently is that they&#8217;ve built a set of habits that give them time, space, and tools to lead. This book is not about grand accomplishments; it&#8217;s about how to find that time, build that space, and refine the tools.</p>

